   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the Netherlands</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving Capacity to Combat False Codling Moth in Rose Production and Export in Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project aims to enhance competitiveness in the Horticulture sector while addressing the urgent issue of False Codling Moth (FCM) interceptions affecting Rose Flower exports to the European Union (EU). Prompted by the EU Commission&apos;s response to increased FCM interceptions from Ethiopia and other East African countries, the project seeks to equip rose growers with skills to identify and manage FCM infestations. It plans to train 10,000 extension workers, EAA inspectors, and farm workers, strengthen farm audit systems at Bole airport, and facilitate knowledge exchange to improve access to EU markets. 
The project operates on a Public-Private Partnership (PPP) model, wherein the private sector collaborates with government authorities like EAA and MoA. Costs are shared among various stakeholders for project implementation. Out of the total budget of 244,776 Euro, 18% will be contributed by affected companies, 17.7% by EHPEA, and the remainder by EKN.</narrative>
  </description>
